{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1911 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5758d0310461bfd516e009c805a283b23a824f4c07cfada34e31f3629866452d" :edb/db-before-t 1910 :edb/db-after-t 1911 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535223 50 #inst "2026-10-08T19:55:05.082Z" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124292 1100 "Find the differential for $f(x,y) = x^2 \\sin y$." 13194139535223 false] [17592186124292 1100 "Find the differential for $f(x,y) = x^2 \\sin y.$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124292 1145 "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndf(x, y) &= (\\frac{∂f}{∂x})dx + (\\frac{∂f}{∂y})dy \\\\\n&= (2x\\sin y)dx + (x^{2}\\cos y)dy \\\\\n&= 2x\\sin ydx + x^{2}\\cos ydy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535223 false] [17592186124292 1145 "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124293 1100 "Given a cone, find the differential $\\textrm d V$ for the volume $V$ of the cone if the radius is $r=6$ and the height is $h = 3$." 13194139535223 false] [17592186124293 1100 "Given a cone, find the differential $\\textrm d V$ for the volume $V$ of the cone if the radius is $r=6$ and the height is $h = 3.$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124293 1145 "The volume of the cone is\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\pi r^2h\n$$\n\nLet's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndV(r, h) &= (\\frac{∂V}{∂r})dr + (\\frac{∂V}{∂h})dh \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}πrhdr + \\frac{1}{3}πr^{2}dh\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=6$ and $h=3$, the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndV(6, 3) &= \\frac{2}{3}π \\cdot 6 \\cdot 3dr + \\frac{1}{3}π \\cdot 6^{2}dh \\\\\n&= 12πdr + 12πdh\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535223 false] [17592186124293 1145 "The volume of the cone is $V = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\pi r^2h.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(r,h) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂V}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi rh\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=6$ and $h=3,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(6,3) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot 6\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{1}{3}\\pi \\cdot {6}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =12\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+12\\pi \\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124294 1100 "If $f (x, y) = xe^y ,$ use the differential $\\textrm d f$ to estimate the change $\\Delta f$ in the value of $f(x, y)$ from $(5,0)$ to $(2,1).$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124294 1100 "If $f (x, y) = xe^y$, use the differential $\\textrm d f$ to estimate the change $\\Delta f$ in the value of $f(x, y)$ from $(5,0)$ to $(2,1)$." 13194139535223 false] [17592186124294 1145 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndf(x, y) &= (\\frac{∂f}{∂x})dx + (\\frac{∂f}{∂y})dy \\\\\n&= e^{y}dx + xe^{y}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(5,0)$, the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndf(5, 0) &= e^{0}dx + 5e^{0}dy \\\\\n&= 1dx + 5dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(5 + \\Delta x, 0 + \\Delta y)$, then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\\Delta f \\approx 1 \\, \\Delta x + 5 \\, \\Delta y\n$$\n\nMoving from $(5,0)$ to $(2,1)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔx &= 2 - 5 =-3, \\\\\nΔy &= 1 - 0 = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nΔf \\mid \\approx 1Δx + 5Δy; \\approx 1 \\cdot (-3) + 5 \\cdot (1); \\approx 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $2$." 13194139535223 false] [17592186124294 1145 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(5,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(5,0) & ={e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x+5{e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+5\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(5 + \\Delta x, 0 + \\Delta y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\\Delta f \\approx 1 \\, \\Delta x + 5 \\, \\Delta y.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(5,0)$ to $(2,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =2-5=-3, \\\\ Δy & =1-0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+5\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $2.$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124295 1100 "Estimate $\\sin (3) + \\cos (0.2)$ using a differential of the function $f(x,y) = \\sin x + \\cos y$ at the point $\\left( \\pi, 0 \\right).$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124295 1100 "Estimate $\\sin (3) + \\cos (0.2)$ using a differential of the function $f(x,y) = \\sin x + \\cos y$ at the point $\\left(\\pi, 0 \\right)$." 13194139535223 false] [17592186124295 1145 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndf(x, y) &= (\\frac{∂f}{∂x})dx + (\\frac{∂f}{∂y})dy \\\\\n&= \\cos xdx - \\sin ydy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\pi, 0 \\right)\n$$\n\nthe differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndf(x, y) &= \\cos πdx - \\sin 0dy \\\\\n&=-1dx + 0dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(\\pi + \\Delta x, 0 + \\Delta y)$, then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\\Delta f \\approx -1 \\, \\Delta x + 0 \\, \\Delta y\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\sin (3) + \\cos (0.2)= f(3,0.2)\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(3,0.2)$.\n\nMoving from $(\\pi, 0)$ to $(3,0.2)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔx &= 3 - π \\approx - 0.141, \\\\\nΔy &= 0.2 - 0 = 0.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nΔf \\mid \\approx - 1(-0.141) + 0(0.2); \\approx 0.141\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.141$. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nf(3, 0.2)\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx f(π, 0) + 0.141 \\\\ \\sin (3) + \\cos (0.2)\\end{vmatrix}\\approx \\sin (π) + \\cos (0) + 0.141; \\approx 0 + 1 + 0.141; \\approx 1.141\n$$" 13194139535223 false] [17592186124295 1145 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $\\left( \\pi, 0 \\right),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\,\\text{d}x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-1\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(\\pi + \\Delta x, 0 + \\Delta y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\\Delta f \\approx  -1  \\,  \\Delta x + 0  \\,  \\Delta y\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sin (3) + \\cos (0.2)= f(3,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(3,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $(\\pi, 0)$ to $(3,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =3-\\pi ≈-0.141, \\\\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈-1(-0.141)+0(0.2) \\\\  & ≈0.141.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.141.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,0.2) & ≈f(\\pi ,0)+0.141 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.2) & ≈\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+0.141 \\\\  & ≈0+1+0.141 \\\\  & ≈1.141.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124486 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338426 13194139535223 true] [17592186124486 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338433 13194139535223 true] [17592186124486 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338440 13194139535223 true] [17592186124486 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338489 13194139535223 true] [17592186124487 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338447 13194139535223 true] [17592186124487 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338454 13194139535223 true] [17592186124487 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338496 13194139535223 true] [17592186124488 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338398 13194139535223 true] [17592186124488 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338405 13194139535223 true] [17592186124488 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338461 13194139535223 true] [17592186124488 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338468 13194139535223 true] [17592186124488 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338475 13194139535223 true] [17592186124489 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338412 13194139535223 true] [17592186124489 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338419 13194139535223 true] [17592186124489 1020 #edb/ref 17592186338482 13194139535223 true] [17592186124967 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535223 true] [17592186124967 1100 "Estimate $\\sqrt[3]{10} + \\sqrt{150}$ using a differential $df$ of the function $f(x, y) = \\sqrt[3]{x} + \\sqrt{y}$ at the point $(8, 144)$. Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535223 false] [17592186124967 1100 "Estimate $\\sqrt[3]{10}+\\sqrt{150}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+\\sqrt{y}$ at the point $(8,144).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535223 true] [17592186124967 1145 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(8,144),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {8}^{2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{144}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(8+Δx,144+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{12}\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{24}\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{10}+\\sqrt{150}=f(10,150),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(10,150).$\n\nMoving from $(8,144)$ to $(10,150),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =10-8=2, \\\\ Δy & =150-144=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{12}(2)+\\frac{1}{24}(6) \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & ≈0.417.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.417.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10,150) & ≈f(8,144)+0.417 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{10}+\\sqrt{150} & ≈\\sqrt[3]{8}+\\sqrt{144}+0.417 \\\\  & ≈2+12+0.417 \\\\  & ≈14.417.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124969 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535223 true] [17592186124969 1100 "Given a sector of a circle, find the differential $\\text{d}S$ for the surface area $S$ if the radius is $r=4$ and the central angle is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124969 1100 "Given a sector of a circle, find the differential $dS$ for the surface area $S$ if the radius is $r = 4$ and the central angle is $θ = \\frac{π}{8}$." 13194139535223 false] [17592186124969 1145 "The area of the sector is $S=\\frac{\\theta }{2}{r}^{2}.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(r,\\theta ) & =(\\frac{∂S}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂S}{∂\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =r\\theta \\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=4$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(4,\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{d}r+8\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124972 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535223 true] [17592186124972 1100 "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{y}^{2}).$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124972 1100 "Find the differential $df$ for the function $f(x, y) = \\ln (x + y^{2})$." 13194139535223 false] [17592186124972 1145 "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{2y\\,\\text{d}y}{x+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124973 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535223 true] [17592186124973 1100 "Given a cylinder, find the differential $\\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ of the cylinder if the radius is $r=2$ and the height is $h=4.$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186124973 1100 "Given a cylinder, find the differential $dV$ for the volume $V$ of the cylinder if the radius is $r = 2$ and the height is $h = 4$." 13194139535223 false] [17592186124973 1145 "The volume of the cylinder is $V=\\pi {r}^{2}h.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(r,h) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂V}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =2\\pi rh\\,\\text{d}r+\\pi {r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=2$ and $h=4,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(2,4) & =2\\pi \\cdot 2\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}r+\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =16\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+4\\pi \\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186125143 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535223 true] [17592186125143 1100 "Estimate $(1.81) \\cdot \\ln (0.91)$ using a differential $df$ of the function $f(x, y) = x\\ln y$ at the point $(2, 1)$." 13194139535223 false] [17592186125143 1100 "Estimate $(1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ at the point $(2,1).$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186125143 1145 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+2\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n(1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91)=f(1.81,0.91),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1.81,0.91).$\n\nMoving from $(2,1)$ to $(1.81,0.91),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.81-2=-0.19, \\\\ Δy & =0.91-1=-0.09.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\cdot (-0.19)+2\\cdot (-0.09) \\\\  & ≈0-0.18 \\\\  & ≈-0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.18.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.81,0.91) & ≈f(2,1)-0.18 \\\\ (1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91) & ≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-0.18 \\\\  & ≈0-0.18 \\\\  & ≈-0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338398 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338399 13194139535223 true] [17592186338398 1089 "q-167166" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338398 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535223 true] [17592186338398 1100 "If $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{2y-z},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(-1,2,3)$ to $(1,0,1).$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338398 1113 #uuid "4ee1f88d-3f94-5266-a2de-5d2046b403ae" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338398 1145 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}x+2x{e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}y-x{e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(-1,2,3),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(-1,2,3) & ={e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot {e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}y-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =e\\,\\text{d}x-2e\\,\\text{d}y+e\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(-1+Δx,2+Δy,3+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈e\\,Δx-2e\\,Δy+e\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(-1,2,3)$ to $(1,0,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-(-1)=2, \\\\ Δy & =0-2=-2 \\\\ Δz & =1-3=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈e\\,Δx-2e\\,Δy+e\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈e\\cdot (2)-2e\\cdot (-2)+e\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & ≈4e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $4e.$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338400 13194139535223 true] [17592186338399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338401 13194139535223 true] [17592186338399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338402 13194139535223 true] [17592186338399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338403 13194139535223 true] [17592186338399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338404 13194139535223 true] [17592186338399 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535223 true] [17592186338399 1041 "selection" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338399 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338403 13194139535223 true] [17592186338399 1110 #uuid "f15be319-2b80-55b8-95ab-a65f4b03e863" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338400 1034 "2e" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338400 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338400 1111 #uuid "1622e7f6-1a2d-5346-9162-2c7d1473e6d5" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338401 1034 "-2e" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338401 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338401 1111 #uuid "d0b817de-88ae-5ad0-92ad-2db6100597db" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338402 1034 "2" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338402 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338402 1111 #uuid "39e40d4c-69e8-5154-be4b-00e6112a28c5" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338403 1034 "4e" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338403 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338403 1111 #uuid "61409cad-8b9f-597f-b024-4f18482d10ac" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338404 1034 "-1" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338404 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338404 1111 #uuid "95266bcb-e5cd-5656-a939-75a0967147f9" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338405 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338406 13194139535223 true] [17592186338405 1089 "q-167168" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338405 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535223 true] [17592186338405 1100 "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz),$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(1,1,1)$ to $(2,3,4).$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338405 1113 #uuid "dd59d308-6e41-5fd0-8b0f-1ea88730848b" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338405 1145 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y+\\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,1,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,1,1) & =1\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+1\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,1+Δy,1+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈Δx+Δy+Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,1,1)$ to $(2,3,4),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =2-1=1, \\\\ Δy & =3-1=2 \\\\ Δz & =4-1=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈Δx+Δy+Δz \\\\  & ≈1+2+3 \\\\  & ≈6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $6.$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338407 13194139535223 true] [17592186338406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338408 13194139535223 true] [17592186338406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338409 13194139535223 true] [17592186338406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338410 13194139535223 true] [17592186338406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338411 13194139535223 true] [17592186338406 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535223 true] [17592186338406 1041 "selection" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338406 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338409 13194139535223 true] [17592186338406 1110 #uuid "e9b71b3b-fc7a-58ab-a94a-6a95f0453e8b" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338407 1034 "8" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338407 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338407 1111 #uuid "156375a1-0669-562c-a408-c68017992149" 1319