:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,\\text{d}y-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,\\text{d}x+(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\,\\text{d}y-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+0\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,0+Δy,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-1=-1, \\\\ Δy & =1-0=1 \\\\ Δz & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (1)+0\\cdot (1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ≈1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $1.$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338477 13194139535223 true] [17592186338476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338478 13194139535223 true] [17592186338476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338479 13194139535223 true] [17592186338476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338480 13194139535223 true] [17592186338476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338481 13194139535223 true] [17592186338476 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535223 true] [17592186338476 1041 "selection" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338476 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338480 13194139535223 true] [17592186338476 1110 #uuid "aa644c6e-aeab-5c7b-84d4-59838b44f193" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338477 1034 "\\pi -2" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338477 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338477 1111 #uuid "ec23fb49-327d-57eb-89f6-6928cecdd1c5" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338478 1034 "2" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338478 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338478 1111 #uuid "1cea4b98-3707-5124-bdb8-96ae4bfb9f37" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338479 1034 "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338479 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338479 1111 #uuid "51eb6df5-a9c5-5682-85e2-ff6354f038f0" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338480 1034 "1" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338480 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338480 1111 #uuid "98679f0f-81fb-568d-a7e3-812f76f7c74e" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338481 1034 "-1" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338481 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338481 1111 #uuid "835f9062-a830-5600-9799-531779ef052f" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338482 1017 #edb/ref 17592186338483 13194139535223 true] [17592186338482 1089 "q-79840" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338482 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535223 true] [17592186338482 1100 "Estimate $\\sqrt{4.1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.9)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ at the point $(4,1).$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338482 1113 #uuid "a7a466ad-861f-5b55-a550-74aad2ec19b3" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338482 1145 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(4,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(4+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δx+1\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4.1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.9)=f(4.1,0.9),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(4.1,0.9).$\n\nMoving from $(4,1)$ to $(4.1,0.9),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =4.1-4=0.1, \\\\ Δy & =0.9-1=-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (0.1)+1(-0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.025-0.1 \\\\  & ≈-0.075.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.075.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4.1,0.9) & ≈f(4,1)-0.075 \\\\ \\sqrt{4.1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.9) & ≈\\sqrt{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-0.075 \\\\  & ≈2-0.075 \\\\  & ≈1.925.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338484 13194139535223 true] [17592186338483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338485 13194139535223 true] [17592186338483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338486 13194139535223 true] [17592186338483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338487 13194139535223 true] [17592186338483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186338488 13194139535223 true] [17592186338483 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535223 true] [17592186338483 1041 "selection" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338483 1104 #edb/ref 17592186338486 13194139535223 true] [17592186338483 1110 #uuid "c608a199-01ac-52ae-ba30-2f6c53784491" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338484 1034 "1.920" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338484 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338484 1111 #uuid "e57e6c52-c828-5c7c-b563-65377c9f2d87" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338485 1034 "1.915" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338485 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338485 1111 #uuid "e218bd7e-ff26-5214-8942-ab669e441e20" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338486 1034 "1.925" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338486 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338486 1111 #uuid "daa6e67f-0752-5b9e-aa6c-be0c42951aac" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338487 1034 "1.930" 13194139535223 true] [17592186338487 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535223 true] [17592186338487 1111 #uuid "a1eba0d9-7e20-5ca0-8446-59e8494f2862" 131941